<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Ngu Foncha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Ngu Foncha was a Cameroonian politician who led the Kamerun National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> and became premier of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a> in 1959. He campaigned for reunification with French-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, which the Southern Cameroons chose in the UN plebiscite of 1961. He then served as vice-president of the Federal Republic of Cameroon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-ngu-foncha.jpg" alt="John Ngu Foncha" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harry Pot · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Ngu_Foncha_(1964).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
